1086 in England has a new king and a new  royal family this new king was william  better known as William the Conqueror  and he was now indisputably in charge  the next year he died which caused  problems mostly for William due to the  whole being dead thing but also because  of the succession Norman inheritance law  was complicated but essentially the  father would give to the eldest son what  he himself had inherited and whatever he  had gained in life he could distribute  however he saw fit  William's eldest son was Robert who  under this law gained Normandy William  second eldest son was also called  William and he inherited England  becoming William the second but he's  better known as William Rufus Henry  Williams third son just got a wad of  cash and no land so being king is  objectively better than being a Duke and  so unsurprisingly Robert was none too  happy with his younger brother now being  his superior so at first both Robert and  William agreed to be each other's heirs  but this agreement broke down in 1088  this was when Robert with the help of  his barons attempted to overthrow  William so that he could be king the  Norman barons were faced with the issue  of owning lands in both Normandy and  England  this meant that if the brothers came to  blows the nobles would certainly have  had their lands confiscated in one place  or the other the barons wanted England  and Normandy to be United politically so  that it was easier for them to  concentrate on the external threats to  their holdings which at this point with  the King of France and the Duke of Anjou  the disagreement between the brothers is  known as the baronial rebellion of 1088  due to the number of nobles that picked  Robert over William it's included  widespread raiding across southern  England but the King eventually won out  by coercing some laws to rejoin him and  by capturing the leader of the revolt  Otto who was banished thereafter  during this rebellion against him  William Rufus had to deal with an  invasion from the Scottish King Malcolm  the 3rd William was busy fighting in  Normandy and so had to return to England  but Malcolm quickly signed a treaty  before any real retaliation could happen  the relationship between England and  Scotland during this period was  complicated and can essentially be  summed up as an uneasy stalemate  Scotland lacked the manpower to conquer  England and Scotland terrain made it too  expensive for an English army to hold  and thus the stalemate was continued  William a malcolm ended up coming to  blows again in 1091 when Malcolm invaded  England but was quickly stopped and  after this the kingdoms entered a period  of prolonged peace and mutual love or  perhaps not since in 1093 the Scottish  ravaged the lands of Northumbria the  Earl of Northumbria presumably annoyed  at his lands being destroyed ambushed  King Malcolm later the same year here at  a place called Ulm wick the ambushed or  King Malcolm and his son and heir Edward  killed thus ending the war  plunging Scotland into a dynastic crisis  Williams issues with his brother were  ended in 1095 not by a military victory  but by a stroke of luck when pope urban  ii began to preach for a crusade to  retake the holy city of jerusalem  Joop Robert now had an easy way out  which could potentially win him his own  lands as well as some glory  Robert thus leased Normandy to William  in order to finance his part in the  First Crusade this fortunate timing by  the church ended one of Williams  disputes but another was still raging  that of his relationship with the church  and specifically Anselm the Archbishop  of Canterbury and some believed that the  church did not answer to the king but to  God through the Pope this belief that  church men owed no allegiance to any  secular power did not sit well with  William boeuf argued for many years and  in 1097 Anselm was exiled and ended up  in Rome the next three years of Williams  reign were fairly secure and his power  remained unchallenged in 1100 Robert was  on his way back from the Crusades having  won glory and also a wealthy wife on the  way back and it seemed that conflict  between Robert and William was looming  in the same year William went hunting  with his younger brother Henry during  which he was killed in an era related  accident which in no way shape or form  looked suspicious before Williams body  was cold Henry had seized Winchester  which held the royal treasury and was  soon after coronated as King Henry the  first Henry had to move quickly to  secure the loyalty of his Lords and  prevent his returning brother from  overthrowing him on the day of his  coronation Henry issued was his called  the charter of liberties in which he  essentially repealed the unpopular  levels of his brother such as a noble  heir having to pay to claim his  inheritance so this is a good place to  explain just how the politics of the  nobility work during the Norman period  the main thing to remember here is that  a law did not own his lands but instead  they were leased from the king and  return for their loyal service and  Payman these Lords could in turn leased  these lands to lesser Lords who would  leased them in turn to the peasants who  worked them the centrality of land to  English life is hard to overstate - the  aristocracy clergy in the monarchy it  was the source of income and authority  but to everyone else King and peasant  alike it provided everything necessary  for medieval life its most obvious  output was food and feeding the kingdom  required the vast majority of the labour  force to be committed to agriculture  even with this commitment the amount of  food produced was not much of a  subsistence agriculture thus made up the  vast majority of England's economy he  had little in the way of raw materials  to export and besides that the most  lucrative of Europe's trade at this time  was centered on the Mediterranean  importantly the fact that everyday life  relied so much in agriculture man that  when there were major crop failures and  were many amend the people simply  starved so back to politics Robert  having returned to Normandy gathered  some of his Nobles and soon after  arrived in England landing in Portsmouth  here many English Nobles including the  powerful Robert of bollène flocked to  Juke Roberts side preparing to overthrow  Henry there was no battle and instead  the brothers agreed that Henry would  remain King but had to pay his brother  an annual sum Henry there moved against  believed seizing his lands in England  and banishing him this was done under  the pretext of belives unauthorized  conquest of parts of Wales than the  booting of castles there the reason for  this is that not just anyone was allowed  to build castles since a rebellious  noble could hold up him one and sieges  were long and expensive soon after this  Juke Robert allied with the exiled Billy  man Henry saw this as a betrayal in an  11:05 he invaded Normandy in 1106 Henry  met his brother at the Battle of  Tannenberg which saw Duke Robert  captured after which he would spend the  last 28 years of his life in captivity  Henry's reign was not a military like  his brother he had some trouble with the  church and some the exiled Archbishop of  Canterbury had returned to England in  1100 and had importantly supported  Henry's claim over his older brothers  this did not mean that the two were good  friends or anything since having spent a  couple of years in Rome and some had  become acquainted with the official  church policy on the appointment of  Bishops the position of the Pope was  that only he could appoint bishop since  Kings were simply laypeople concerned  with lesser earthly things and that the  Pope's were concerned with greater  heavenly issues unsurprisingly this did  not please Henry since appointing people  to important church positions was an  important way of making allies there was  some bickering between the two and  despite the king officially agreeing to  the demands his say-so was still very  important in deciding who would be  appointed he was only a technical  concession but importantly Henry had  agreed that he was a member of the laity  and that the Pope was above him in  authority so back to politics in Henry  having imprisoned his brother was now  also the duke of normandy meaning that  the main threats to his power were  external the two major threats were  lured the sick the King of France and  folk the fifth the Duke of Anjou the  former Duke Robert had a son William who  felt the normandy was rightfully his and  he sought the help of the other laws to  retake his birthright in 1118 with the  help of the Count of Flanders he led a  rebellion in Normandy which saw most of  the Duchy full but it dissipated quickly  after the Count of Flanders was injured  in the siege in the next year Louis the  six of France invaded but Henry led an  army against him and the French were  defeated at the Battle of Bream you'll  to finance this war immense taxes were  raised including the continuation of the  day in gold established under the  previous anglo-saxon monarchs henry was  notable for being  with the kingdom's finances the  evaluation of which was done with the  chequered piece of cloth called the  Exchequer by the way this is why the  modern minister in charge of the economy  is called the Chancellor of the  Exchequer Henry had just managed to  avoid catastrophe in losing Normandy but  his reign was made much more complicated  when his son William drowned in the  English Channel after his ship sank this  left Henry with no legitimate male heirs  only a daughter Matilda  in 1125 Matilda was left widowed after a  husband the Holy Roman Emperor died and  returned to England where King Henry  appointed her as his heir Matilda is  often referred to his Empress during  this period but after her husband died  she no longer held the title Henry  married her to the son of count folke  Evangel man called Geoffrey Plantagenet  Henry soon after fell out with Geoffrey  Matilda which meant that upon Henry's  death in 1135 a succession crisis would  break out so there was two potential  heirs for the English throne  the first was of course Geoffrey who at  the time of Henry's death was in Anjou  the other was Henry's nephew Stephen the  count of bolon given Henry's arguments  with Geoffrey Matilda the English Nobles  weren't too keen on them where Stephen  was very popular  fortunately for Stephen he was much  closer to England than his rivals  meaning he could act first which he did  he soon after arrived in London and  secured the support of the major Lords  and on December the 22nd he was crowned  King of England am a Duke of Normandy  major problems didn't begin for Steven  until 1138 when Robert the Earl of  Gloucester revolted against him  declaring for Matilda to make matters  worse Geoffrey who was now the count of  Anjou since his father had left to  become King of Jerusalem invaded  Normandy at the same time in the North  King David the first of Scotland invaded  in annex Carlisle this period of English  history is often referred to as the  anarchy but in many respects it was more  routine war of succession there were  rebelling lords and Aerys which English  Authority was shattered but ultimately  was no more anarchic than any of the  Civil War previous to this during the  Anarchy Stephen made several errors the  first was allowing Matilda to slip  through his grasp after she landed to  arendelle here and met with Earl Robert  of Gloucester in Bristol in 1139 his  next mistake was at the Battle of  Lincoln an 1141 in which instead of  retreating when the battle was going  against him Stephen continued to fight  and ended up getting yourself captured  and imprisoned they should have been the  end of the Civil War and Stephens reign  but that would have required the other  side to not make mistakes also which  they did  Matilda rented London and swiftly 